Which terms apply to which files

The MIT license above covers das3js's own code: everything in this
repository outside deps/, and these files of the npm package:

  build.wasm32/das3js.mjs
  build.wasm32/das-catalog-tree.mjs
  build.wasm32/das-source-form.mjs

build.wasm32/das2c.mjs is generated by the Emscripten compiler and holds
its JavaScript runtime, available under the MIT license or the University
of Illinois/NCSA Open Source License.  See https://emscripten.org.

build.wasm32/das2c.wasm is compiled from das3js's C code (glue/, src/)
statically linked with das2C, the Core Das C Library, which is distributed
under the GNU Lesser General Public License version 2.1 only.  The file as
a whole is therefore distributed under the terms of that license; the
das3js code within it remains available under the MIT license as well.
das2c.wasm also contains these, under their own permissive terms:

  Expat XML parser               MIT license         https://libexpat.github.io
  zlib compression library       zlib license        https://zlib.net
  tomlc17 TOML parser, CK Tan    MIT license         https://github.com/cktan/tomlc17
  json.h, Neil Henning           Unlicense           https://github.com/sheredom/json.h
  UTF-8 routines, Jeff Bezanson  public domain
  TT2000 time conversion         NASA CDF license    https://cdf.gsfc.nasa.gov

Their license texts, and das2C's, are in the LICENSES directory next to
this file.  das2c.wasm does not contain FFTW or any other GPL code.

Source code.  das2C's source is at https://github.com/das-developers/das2C.
Each das3js release builds against the das2C commit recorded as the
deps/das2C git submodule at that release's tag in
https://github.com/das-developers/das3js, which holds all of the source
for das2c.wasm.  To use a modified das2C, rebuild with `make` (see
notes/build.md) and replace das2c.wasm and das2c.mjs with the result;
nothing else in a page or application needs to change.

Bundling and minifying.  Building an application with a bundler such as
Vite, webpack, Rollup or esbuild, which combines and minifies the
JavaScript above and copies das2c.wasm out under a new, hashed name, is
allowed by all of these licenses.  None of them asks that the
application's own code be published, or placed under any of these
licenses, because it is bundled with das3js.  What they ask of an
application that is distributed, put on a public web server included:

  * Keep the notices.  Most minifiers, Vite's and webpack's among them,
    drop comments, so the notices travel as a file: include this file
    and the LICENSES directory with the application, or list das3js in
    its third-party notices and link to them.  Vite does the listing when
    its build.license option is on, writing .vite/license.md, which
    includes this file.

  * Keep das2c.wasm a separate file, as bundlers do by default, so that
    it can be replaced with one built from a modified das2C as described
    above, and tell users where its source is, for example by linking to
    the das3js repository.  The application's terms of use must not
    forbid that replacement, or reverse engineering to debug it.

This section is a plain-language summary; the license texts govern.
